WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.250
so we can do actual work.

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:04.190
And one of those things is called
memory or random access memory.

00:00:04.190 --> 00:00:08.135
And random access memory
looks, physically, like this.

00:00:08.135 --> 00:00:14.150
This is a chip that you might slide into
a little slot inside of your computer

00:00:14.150 --> 00:00:18.050
specifically on the so-called
motherboard, again, a big greenish

00:00:18.050 --> 00:00:20.779
board into which all of your
computer's components connect.

00:00:20.779 --> 00:00:23.570
And there's usually a little divot
here, a little bump to make sure

00:00:23.570 --> 00:00:26.611
you put it in the right way-- instead
this way, instead of, for instance,

00:00:26.611 --> 00:00:27.260
that way.

00:00:27.260 --> 00:00:30.440
And then on this green circuit
board are all these various chips

00:00:30.440 --> 00:00:32.510
that actually store your data.

00:00:32.510 --> 00:00:37.700
Specifically, they store your data in
a volatile way, only when the power is

00:00:37.700 --> 00:00:40.190
on, when your laptop has
battery and is running

00:00:40.190 --> 00:00:42.740
or when your laptop is
plugged in and is running.

00:00:42.740 --> 00:00:45.260
Which is to say, when you
double-click an icon on the screen

00:00:45.260 --> 00:00:49.220
or run some program, open
some file, those files

00:00:49.220 --> 00:00:54.050
are loaded into the little black
chips that you see on this stick here.

00:00:54.050 --> 00:00:56.750
They're loaded into RAM
or Random Access Memory.

00:00:56.750 --> 00:00:58.370
And this memory is pretty fast.

00:00:58.370 --> 00:01:00.920
And it is where those
files and programs live,

00:01:00.920 --> 00:01:04.250
so long as you are using them
at a given moment in time.

00:01:04.250 --> 00:01:07.370
Now other devices have slightly
different looking sticks of RAM.

00:01:07.370 --> 00:01:09.500
This is a smaller stick
here that's actually

00:01:09.500 --> 00:01:13.550
found, typically, in laptops or certain
desktop models, but it's the same idea.

00:01:13.550 --> 00:01:15.510
It's simply a different form factor.

00:01:15.510 --> 00:01:18.020
Now you can actually
see in your Mac or PC

00:01:18.020 --> 00:01:20.169
sometimes exactly how much
RAM you have, especially

00:01:20.169 --> 00:01:22.460
if you bought the computer
without knowing this concept

00:01:22.460 --> 00:01:24.930
or were given the computer
and didn't think to ask.

00:01:24.930 --> 00:01:28.910
In fact, on Windows, if you open
up the so-called Task Manager

00:01:28.910 --> 00:01:33.110
and look at the CPU tab, you might
see a little something like this.

00:01:33.110 --> 00:01:36.380
You'll see one, a nice little chart
that shows you what your CPU has

00:01:36.380 --> 00:01:38.120
been doing over the past few seconds.

00:01:38.120 --> 00:01:39.950
And the higher these peaks
are, the busier it was.

00:01:39.950 --> 00:01:42.033
Maybe you double-clicked
an icon or played a video

00:01:42.033 --> 00:01:44.570
or sent a really big email
or something like that.

00:01:44.570 --> 00:01:47.990
But more interesting, down
here, is the speed of your CPU.

00:01:47.990 --> 00:01:50.600
This one is 1.94 gigahertz.

00:01:50.600 --> 00:01:54.800
And a gigahertz means 1
billion things per second.

00:01:54.800 --> 00:01:59.360
So that's 1.94 gigahertz
means that this computer, this CPU

00:01:59.360 --> 00:02:03.590
can do 1.94 billion things per second--

00:02:03.590 --> 00:02:05.330
additions, subtractions,
multiplications,

00:02:05.330 --> 00:02:08.040
printing to the screen,
any number of other things.

00:02:08.040 --> 00:02:12.500
But if we look, odds are this is
marketed, frankly, as 2 gigahertz,

00:02:12.500 --> 00:02:15.020
and maybe it has burst technology
whereby it can actually

00:02:15.020 --> 00:02:18.140
go even a little faster,
maximum speed 2.81

00:02:18.140 --> 00:02:23.242
gigahertz by maybe using a little
more energy for some amount of time

00:02:23.242 --> 00:02:24.950
and therefore generating
a bit more heat.

00:02:24.950 --> 00:02:28.730
But you'll see that there's only one
socket in the computer from which I

00:02:28.730 --> 00:02:31.560
took this screenshot from Windows,
which means there's one CPU.

00:02:31.560 --> 00:02:35.720
But that CPU has two cores, which means
that the computer can do literally

00:02:35.720 --> 00:02:39.987
two things at once because it has
really two brains inside of that device

00:02:39.987 --> 00:02:41.570
that we saw a picture of a moment ago.

00:02:41.570 --> 00:02:43.880
But logically, it actually seems four.

00:02:43.880 --> 00:02:49.160
So this particular windows computer
has a CPU with two cores, each of which

00:02:49.160 --> 00:02:51.650
supports that technology
called hyperthreading, which

00:02:51.650 --> 00:02:56.120
means that each of those cores will
present itself to the Windows operating

00:02:56.120 --> 00:02:57.590
system as though it's two.

00:02:57.590 --> 00:03:00.770
Which is to say each core will
do two things simultaneously,

00:03:00.770 --> 00:03:05.120
thereby giving me four logical
processors or, really, the ability

00:03:05.120 --> 00:03:07.410
to do 4 total things at once.

00:03:07.410 --> 00:03:10.220
So perhaps lower level detail
than is perhaps germane,

00:03:10.220 --> 00:03:13.550
but these are the kinds of things
that you end up paying for in a store

00:03:13.550 --> 00:03:15.444
or when you buy a computer online.

00:03:15.444 --> 00:03:18.110
This is what distinguishes a less
expensive computer from a more

00:03:18.110 --> 00:03:22.400
expensive computer is just
how fast the CPU or CPUs are

00:03:22.400 --> 00:03:25.460
and just how many cores
the CPU has and, therefore,

00:03:25.460 --> 00:03:29.480
just how much work you can get
done at a given moment in time

00:03:29.480 --> 00:03:31.930
or just how quickly you
can work more generally.

00:03:31.930 --> 00:03:35.100
Now, in a Mac, if you open
up your System Profiler,

00:03:35.100 --> 00:03:38.310
you'll see a different kind of
interface, but similar information.

00:03:38.310 --> 00:03:40.560
This is taken here from a MacBook Pro.

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:45.750
You can see that this CPU
is 3.1 gigahertz, so faster,

00:03:45.750 --> 00:03:50.010
but it has one processor,
total number of cores is two.

00:03:50.010 --> 00:03:54.240
And so, here, we see no mention of
hyperthreading, which generally windows

00:03:54.240 --> 00:03:58.560
is a bit more detailed on, but this does
mean that this CPU is pretty darn fast,

00:03:58.560 --> 00:04:00.990
3.1 billion things per second.

00:04:00.990 --> 00:04:05.970
And it actually has two cores, so it
can do at least two things at once.

00:04:05.970 --> 00:04:06.570
All right.

00:04:06.570 --> 00:04:10.170
So that's my CPU, which does all
the thinking and all of the work.

00:04:10.170 --> 00:04:13.620
That's RAM or random access memory
where all of my data and programs

00:04:13.620 --> 00:04:16.690
are stored while I am using them.

00:04:16.690 --> 00:04:18.550
So what else do I need?

00:04:18.550 --> 00:04:22.200
Well, where are those programs and
files stored when the power is not on

00:04:22.200 --> 00:04:26.800
or when my battery is dead or when
my computer is not even plugged in.

00:04:26.800 --> 00:04:30.570
Now, odds are, if your computer is like
mine, you don't lose all of your files

00:04:30.570 --> 00:04:32.880
and all of your programs just
because your battery dies

00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:35.505
or you move a computer, and
therefore, unplug it from the wall.

00:04:35.505 --> 00:04:37.320
It would not be a very useful device.

00:04:37.320 --> 00:04:41.850
Computers do have nonvolatile
memory that sticks around

00:04:41.850 --> 00:04:44.910
even when the power is lost, but
it uses a different technology

00:04:44.910 --> 00:04:46.350
than RAM for that.

00:04:46.350 --> 00:04:50.160
It tends to use a disk or a
hard disk, as in this case.

00:04:50.160 --> 00:04:53.000
So this is a device that a PC desktop
or Mac desktop might have inside of it.
